\subsubsection{Explicit construction for the 1D harmonic oscillator}
As discussed in~\ref{subsec:analytic_interaction}, the harmonic oscillator problem is separable in the center-of-mass and the relative parts. Therefore, we may focus on the effective interaction between two orbitals in the relative coordinates. The corresponding effective interaction is constructed by exchanging $v_{aa'}$ in \equref{matrix_element_int} by the effective interaction (cf. \equref{veff_definition}). All other parts in the FCI implementation stay exactly as in the case of the bare interaction. 


To construct the effective interaction, we notice that the relative wavefunction for two particles in a hamornic oscillator is given by~\cite{Busch1998}
\begin{equation}
	\label{eq:busch_eigenfunctions}
	f_\nu(r) = \mathcal{N}_\nu \e^{-r^2/2}\mathcal{U}(-\nu,\tfrac{1}{2},r^2)
\end{equation}
with $\mathcal{U}$ denoting the Tricomi function and $\nu = \tfrac{\varepsilon}{2} - \tfrac{1}{4}$. The normalization coefficient reads
\begin{equation}
	\mathcal{N}_\nu = \left[\int\d x\ \e^{-x^2}\mathcal{U}(-\nu,\tfrac{1}{2},x^2)^2\right]^{-1} = \sqrt{\frac{1}{\pi}\frac{\Gamma(-\nu+\tfrac{1}{2})\Gamma(-\nu)}{\digamma(-\nu+\tfrac{1}{2})-\digamma(-\nu)}}
\end{equation}
with the digamma function $\digamma(x) = \Gamma'(x)/\Gamma(x)$.
The eigenenergies $\varepsilon_n$ are given by the solutions of the equation~\cite{Busch1998}
\begin{equation}
	\label{eq:busch_formula}
	\frac{\Gamma(-\nu_n+\tfrac{1}{2})}{\Gamma{-\nu_n}} = -\frac{g}{2}.
\end{equation}

