\label{Bcube}
\end{eqnarray}
Our choice of conjugation of certain operators in both cubes is completely innocuous since all the lattice operators are Hermitian. This is
simply a convenience for the description in terms of continuum fields, leading to more symmetrical forms for the $T$-vectors that will be constructed below. Which operators are conjugated or not reflects assignments of charges at the corners of the cube. According to the choices in Fig.\ref{fig:haah-cubes-paulis}, under an inversion about the center, the charge arrangement of the cube $C^{(1)}$ is mapped into the one of the cube $C^{(2)}$ and vice-versa. This symmetry mimics the self-duality between the cube operators. 


